% TRIO, third part, bars 209-240.
% c022: 225-236 the T head steps B-C sharp-B over the plagal IV (Ob 1, Cl 1, Bsn 1; Hn 3 A): I-IV-I with the 5-6-5 neighbour (A-S PDF 195-196).
% 209-224: T returns, forte espressivo: Vln I with Fl 1 at the octave above Cl 1; Vln II and violas off-beat; the cellos' countermelody;
%   harp; Hn 1-2 sustain the harmony (no unison horns: the high horn unison is kept for the finale).
% 225-240: codetta over the E pedal with plagal A-E: T's head falls through Ob 1 (225), Cl 1 (229), Bsn 1 (233; c003: Hn 1 keeps T for the coda, now at 341; c103 renumbered); poco rit. 237-240.

% c061: 221-222 T's cadence with the tied V4-3 suspension E-D sharp, as at 165-166 and 181-182 (see tA.ily; Aldwell-Schachter PDF 352 sec. 8).
% c069: Fl 1 rests 209-216 and enters at 217 for the third phrase's climb to A6 (Adler II PDF 37: hold a colour back for a specific event; PDF 156: a rest before a colour enters makes it clearly perceived; PDF 165: sustained doublings at pitch tire the ear and lose colour). Fl 2's G sharp at 209 (c050/c065) is kept.
% c093: 215-216 the raised 4th of V7/V (A sharp) only in the violas: cellos and Bsn 2 hold C sharp 3, Hn 1 holds C sharp 5 (was A sharp 4, an octave over the violas' A sharp 3,
%   both rising to B together at 216); Hn 1 still reaches B4 on beat 2 of 216, the breath and slurs unchanged (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 14, PDF 214: the raised 4th is not doubled).
% c098: 221-222 the cadential 4-3 shaded <> as at 165-166 (Aldwell-Schachter PDF 348, 350).
tCFluteOne = { R2.*8 | cis'''2(\mf\< dis'''4 | e'''4 gis'''4 a'''4~ | a'''4.\f\> gis'''8 fis'''4 | cis'''4 b''4 a''4) | gis''4\mf( b''4 e'''4\<~ | e'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f\> dis'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mf fis'''4 | e'''2.~ | e'''2)\> r4\! | R2.*16 | }
% c158: 225-236 each codetta statement of T's head (Ob 1, Cl 1, Bsn 1) swells to its third bar (C sharp 6, C sharp 5, C natural 4) and falls back (hidden mp/p targets, c138): Sebesky Ex. 2A, Ex. 269 (PDF 18, 205): every line its own < > arc; PDF 207: carry one bar into the next.
tCOboeOne = { R2.*16 | gis''2(\p a''4 | b''2.\< | cis'''2.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | b''2-\tweak stencil ##f \p) r4 | R2.*12 | }
tCClOne = { gis'2(\f a'4 | b'2 e''4 | dis''4. cis''8 b'4 | a'4 gis'2) | a'2( b'4 | cis''4 fis''2 | e''4. dis''8 cis''4 | ais'4 b'2) | cis''2(\< dis''4 | e''4 gis''4 a''4~ | a''4.-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> gis''8 fis''4 | cis''4 b'4 a'4) | gis'4-\tweak stencil ##f \f( b'4 e''4\<~ | e''4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> dis''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f fis''4 | e''2.~ | e''2)\> r4\! | R2.*4 | gis'2(\p a'4 | b'2.\< | cis''2.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | b'2-\tweak stencil ##f \p) r4 | R2.*8 | }
% c050: 209 Fl 2 and Cl 2 end the motto-tail line B-A sharp-A (206-208) on its G sharp, the first note of Fl 1 and Cl 1's T (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 125-126:
%   in a transferred phrase the last note of one part coincides with the first note of the next), so the quotation resolves in its own voice, as Hn 1's at 357-360. The G sharp is re-attacked with T's first note (no slur across: the part combiner merges the unison).
tCFluteTwo = { gis''2\mf r4 | R2.*31 | }
% c127: 229-232 Cl 2 rests and Hn 4 holds the E pedal (E3, pp) under Cl 1's statement of T's head: Cl 2's E4 lay a third under the clarinet solo in its own colour,
%   so Cl 1 entered as one of a pair while Ob 1 (225) and Bsn 1 (233, c126) state the head alone (Adler II PDF 267: Mozart writes no orchestral flutes beside the
%   solo flute and holds the tonic in the horns and basses; PDF 264: no orchestral horns beside the solo horn; PDF 274: registral distance). Cl 2 keeps 225-228 under the oboe.
tCClTwo = { gis'2\f r4 | R2.*15 | e'2.\pp | e'2. | e'2. | e'2. | R2.*4 | R2.*8 | }
% c023: Hn 1 holds G sharp 237-240 (moved from the retransition's 241-244), so the inflection G sharp -> G natural falls on 241, the first bar of the
%   retransition (A&S PDF 584-586, modulation by chromatic inflection); timpani retune G sharp -> G during the rests at 225.
% c042: 235 the third plagal of the codetta is iv (A minor, mixture), not IV: Bsn 1 B-C natural-B (flat 6 as upper neighbour to 5), harp C natural;
%   the change of mode varies the third statement and prepares the E minor of 241 (A-S PDF 387, 390 Ex. 23-5, 392-393 sec. 5).
% c014: 209-224 Bsn 1 an inner part between the bass and Hn 1-2 (2 bassoons + 2 horns in four parts, the 7ths in the bassoon; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 100, 108)
tCBsnOne = { \partCombineAutomatic e'2.--(\mf | e'2.-- | fis'2.-- | e'2.-- | e'2.-- | e'2.-- | e'2.-- | dis'2) r4 | cis'2.--(\< | b2.-- | a2.--\f\> | a2.-- | b2.--\mf | a2.-- | gis2.--\> | gis2)\! r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | gis2(\p a4 | b2.\< | c'2.-\tweak stencil ##f \mp\> | b2-\tweak stencil ##f \p) r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c087: 209-224 Bsn 2 doubles the cellos' countermelody in unison (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 76-77: 'Cellos + Fag. is among the best unison pairs, the wind strengthens the string tone and the strings predominate;
%   PDF 78-79: a low melody in the cellos doubled by a bassoon, the basses undoubled). It had duplicated the double basses' bass an octave higher, in the cellos' register,
%   crossing their line at 209, 213 and 216. The bass stays in the basses (arco) and the harp's left hand. Both bassoons mf, a degree under the cellos' f (the shared staff prints one set).
% c129: 219 Bsn 2 holds A3 (was A3-G sharp 3 with the cellos): the cellos' passing G sharp 3 no longer makes a semitone with Bsn 1's A3 on the shared bassoon staff;
%   the second sounds between different colours (cello against bassoons and violas), Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102, 108.
tCBsnTwo = { \partCombineAutomatic b,4(\mf e2 | gis2. | fis2 a4 | gis2 fis4) | e4( cis2~ | cis2 fis4 | cis2. | dis2 b,4) \breathe | e4(\< gis2~ | gis4 e2 | a2.\f\> | fis2.) | b,2(\mf cis4 | a,4 b,2~ | b,2.~\> | b,2)\! r4 | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c088: 209-224 Hn 1-2 and Bsn 1 (the soft four-part choir of c014/c047) under phrase slurs split at the existing breath (216), tenuto on repeated notes; 233-236 Hn 3-4 likewise. The trio's first horn pad (153-168) is already tied by phrase (Adler I PDF 309, 311-312).
% c094: 218-219 Hn 1 G sharp 4 (held from 217) and A4 (were B4 and C sharp 5), so Hn 1-2 hold thirds (E-G sharp, F sharp-A) under the strings' tune instead of open fifths
%   (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 97, Remark 4: with the top part in the strings the wind's middle parts take 2nds, 7ths, 3rds or 6ths, not open 4ths and 5ths; PDF 90 rule 4); 218 now sounds the I6's third in the middle.
tCHnOne = { b'2.--(\p | b'2.-- | b'2.-- | b'2.-- | cis''2.-- | cis''2.-- | cis''2.~ | cis''4 b'2) \breathe | gis'2.--(\< | gis'2.-- | a'2.--\mf\> | cis''2.-- | b'2.--\p | b'2.-- | gis'2.-- | gis'2.--) | R2.*12 | gis'2.~\pp | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2 r4 | }
% c054: 212 Hn 2 enters on beat 2 with the tune's G sharp 4: the held G sharp 4 sounded under the appoggiatura A4 of Cl 1 and Vln II in the same octave
%   (Adler PDF 255: the melody must come through and not be obscured by the harmony; PDF 243: sustained winds placed apart from the theme; as c039 at 197-200).
% c129: 209 Hn 2 E4 (was G sharp 4): T's lower octave (Vln II, Cl 1) starts G sharp 4-A4 in the horn's register, so the held G sharp 4 doubled it and then rubbed against its A4
%   (the clash c039 removed at 197-200 and c047 at 229; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 116, 132-133). Hn 1-2 open on the fifth E-B (the first statement's drone, 153) and take the third from 210.
tCHnTwo = { e'2.--(\p | gis'2.-- | fis'2.--) | r4 gis'2--( | a'2.-- | e'2.-- | e'2.-- | dis'2.--) \breathe | e'2.--(\< | e'2.-- | fis'2.--\mf\> | fis'2.-- | gis'2.--\p | fis'2.-- | e'2.-- | e'2.--) | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | }
% c051: 233-236 Hn 3 E4 (was B3-A3-B3, in unison with Bsn 1's B3 at 234 and 236): the horns frame the bassoon's T head an octave apart and Bsn 1 is p,
%   a degree above the pp horns (Sebesky PDF 212: the background supplies chord tones not in the solo line; PDF 219: do not cover a soloist's weaker register).
% c126: 233-236 Hn 3 rests (was E4 pp just above Bn 1's G sharp 3-C4 statement of T's head): the bassoon in its middle register blends and is swallowed when enclosed by
%   instruments of its own range (Adler I PDF 226); Hn 4 keeps the E3 pedal below it, as the violas lie ppp under Bn 1 at 345 (c078). Hn 3 and Hn 4 re-enter pp at 237.
tCHnThree = { R2. | R2.*23 | R2.*4 | b2.~\pp | b2.~ | b2.~ | b2 r4 | }
tCHnFour = { R2. | R2.*19 | e2.--(\pp | e2.-- | e2.-- | e2.--) | e2.--( | e2.-- | e2.-- | e2.--) | e2.~\pp | e2.~ | e2.~ | e2 r4 | }
tCTimp = { e,4\f r4 r4 | R2.*3 | R2.*11 | e,4\p r4 r4 | R2.^\markup \small \italic \column { "muta G♯ in G" "medium sticks" } | R2.*11 | e,2.:32\pp~ | e,2.:32~ | e,2.:32~ | e,4 r4 r4 | }
% c153: 225-236 the codetta's accompaniment thins by four-bar unit while T's head falls Ob 1 -> Cl 1 -> Bsn 1: harp off-beats on beats 2 and 3 (225-228), beat 2 only (229-232),
%   one chord with the downbeat (233-236), so the pulse goes 3 -> 2 -> 1 per bar into the poco rit. at 237 and the stretch winds down instead of standing still
%   (Adler II PDF 267-268, Mozart K. 313/iii: the accompaniment figure changes from phrase to phrase; PDF 288, Haydn: the doubling changes in the second phrase; PDF 268-269: a sparser texture
%   for the entry). Pitches, register (c047) and the iv's C natural at 235 (c042) unchanged.
tCHarpR = { R2._\markup \small "A♮" | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | R2. | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <a cis' e'>4 <a cis' e'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 <gis b e'>4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 r4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 r4 | r4 <a cis' e'>4 r4 | r4 <gis b e'>4 r4 | <e' gis' b'>4 r4 r4 | <e' gis' b'>4_\markup \small "C♮" r4 r4 | <e' a' c''>4 r4 r4 | <e' gis' b'>4 r4 r4 | <gis b e'>2.\arpeggio | R2. | R2. | \tag #'part \once \override MultiMeasureRest.minimum-length = #16 R2._\markup \small \italic "étouffez" | }  % c165: damp at the end of 240, before the harp part's page turn at 240/241 (was on 241, after the turn; MOLA PDF 7)
tCHarpL = { e,4\mf r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4_\markup \small "A♯" r4 | ais,,4 r4 r4 | ais,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4_\markup \small "A♮" r4 | cis,4 r4 r4 | gis,,4 r4 r4 | fis,,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | b,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4\pp r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,2. | R2.*3 | }
tCVlnOne = { gis''2(\f^\markup \italic "espress." a''4 | b''2 e'''4) | dis'''4.( cis'''8 b''4) | a''4( gis''2) | a''2( b''4 | cis'''4 fis'''2) | e'''4.( dis'''8 cis'''4 | ais''4 b''2) | cis'''2(\< dis'''4 | e'''4 gis'''4) a'''4~( | a'''4.\ff\> gis'''8 fis'''4 | cis'''4 b''4 a''4) | gis''4\f( b''4 e'''4\<~ | e'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> dis'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f fis'''4) | e'''2.~ | e'''2\> r4\! | R2.*16 | }
tCVlnTwo = { gis'2(\f a'4 | b'2 e''4) | dis''4.( cis''8 b'4) | a'4( gis'2) | a'2( b'4 | cis''4 fis''2) | e''4.( dis''8 cis''4 | ais'4 b'2) | cis''2(\< dis''4 | e''4 gis''4) a''4~( | a''4.\ff\> gis''8 fis''4 | cis''4 b'4 a'4) | gis'4\f( b'4 e''4\<~ | e''4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff\> dis''4-\tweak stencil ##f \f fis''4) | e''2.~ | e''2\> r4\! | b'2.\pp | b'2. | a'2. | b'2. | e''2. | e''2. | e''2. | e''2. | b'2. | b'2. | a'2. | b'2. | b'2.~ | b'2.~ | b'2.~ | b'2 r4 | }
% c027: 209-224 viola off-beats in the trio's first voicing (B3-E4, as at 169-184), below the tune's lower octave in Vln II and Cl 1 (were E4-B4, above it; Adler II PDF 274, 279)
% c089: 212-213 violas E4-D4 on beat 3 of the tune's fourth bar, then C sharp 4: a passing seventh, I - V7/IV - IV, so the second and third statements of the
%   trio tune are not harmonised exactly like the first (153-156 stays plain). A-S PDF 418-419 (Ex. 25-1: applied and passing applied chords in a plain theme), PDF 399 (the 7th resolves
%   down by step). The temporary leading tone G sharp is in the tune only on beat 3 (cellos have moved to F sharp, PDF 419: not doubled); F sharp (9th) -> E and D -> C sharp in 173/213.
% c157: 214-215 the third statement's second phrase rises A-A sharp-A sharp-B in the bass (double basses, contrabassoon): V6/5 of V (F sharp 7 over A sharp) for two bars
%   instead of ii - V7/V over A-F sharp-F sharp-B, so the forte return reaches the same B at 216 by another chord (Sebesky PDF 19, Ex. 3/3A: do not harmonise a melody the same way twice;
%   PDF 20: a substitution for a lift). 153-160 and 169-176 keep ii (c089: the first statement plain, later ones coloured). A sharp only in the bass (c093, A-S PDF 214); the 7th E4
%   in Bsn 1 and Hn 2, resolving to D sharp 4 at 216 (Hn 1-2 in sixths C sharp 5/E4, B4/D sharp 4, c094); violas C sharp 4/F sharp 4; cellos and Bsn 2 C sharp 3 (minim)-F sharp 3 on beat 3, after the tune has reached F sharp (no A natural, no octaves with its C sharp-F sharp);
%   harp left hand C sharp 2 (its A natural pedal stays).
% c157: 209-224 cellos' countermelody mf < f > mf with the bassoons (was f throughout, the tune's own level): Sebesky PDF 13 (the ear goes to the primary element, not to a counter-line),
%   as the second statement (169: tune mp, cellos p) and c099's one-level rule. Answers c087's 'a degree under the cellos': in the cello-bassoon unison the strings predominate anyway (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 76).
tCVla = { r4 <b e'>4--(\mf^\markup \italic "div." <b e'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b e'>4--) | r4 <a dis'>4--( <a dis'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b d'>4--) | r4 <a cis'>4--( <a cis'>4--) | r4 <cis' fis'>4--( <cis' fis'>4--) | r4 <cis' fis'>4--( <cis' fis'>4--) | r4 <b dis'>4--( <b dis'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--(\< <b e'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--( <b e'>4--) | r4 <cis' e'>4--(\f\> <cis' e'>4--) | r4 <a e'>4--( <a e'>4--) | r4 <b e'>4--(\mf <b e'>4--) | r4 <a fis'>4--( <a fis'>4--) | r4 <gis e'>4--(\> <gis e'>4--) | r4 <gis e'>4--( <gis e'>4--) | gis'2.\pp^\markup \italic "unis." | gis'2. | e'2. | gis'2. | b2. | b2. | a2. | b2. | gis'2. | gis'2. | e'2. | gis'2. | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2.~ | gis'2 r4 | }
tCVc = { b,4(\mf e2 | gis2.) | fis2( a4 | gis2 fis4) | e4( cis2~ | cis2 fis4) | cis2.( | dis2 b,4) | e4(\< gis2~ | gis4 e2) | a2(\f\> gis4 | fis2.) | b,2(\mf cis4 | a,4 b,2) | b,2.~\upbow\> | b,2 r4 | e4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | a,4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | a,4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | a,4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | e4 r4 r4 | }
% c033: 209-224 double basses arco, sustaining the bass under T's forte return (pizzicato at 153 and 169): the third statement is scored differently
%   by alteration of detail (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 118 d); the harp's left hand keeps the plucked downbeat; pizz. again at 225 after a beat's rest;
%   216 B-F sharp (fifth of V on beat 3), so the sustained bass does not move B-C sharp in octaves with T's B5-C sharp 6 into 217.
% c101: 209-224 the contrabassoon doubles the basses' bass line in unison, a degree softer: in T's forte return the cellos and Bsn 2 carry the countermelody,
%   so the bass was in the basses alone (with the harp's plucked downbeats). Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 56: no phrase of importance in the double basses without the
%   support of cellos or bassoons; Ex. 11 (Golden Cockerel), double basses + double bassoon; PDF 54: the double bass is dull in tone. Unison, so nothing crosses the cellos (c094).
% c114: 213-222 the contrabassoon doubles the basses an octave higher (sounding A2-F#2-C#3-G#2-B2), back in unison on E at 223: the bass fell to F#1/A1
%   with nothing up to A3/C#3 (219: F#1-A3, 27 semitones); Rimsky-Korsakov appendix PDF 505-507 (the bass always has its octave) and PDF 125. Under the cellos' line, no crossing (c087).
tCCbsn = { e,2.--(\mp | e,2.-- | b,,2.-- | e,2.--) | a,2.--( | ais,2.-- | ais,2.-- | b,2 fis,4) \breathe | cis2.--(\< | gis,2.-- | fis,2.--\mf\> | a,2.-- | b,2.--\mp | b,2.-- | e,2.~\> | e,2)\! r4 | R2.*16 | }
tCCb = { e,2.\mf^\markup \italic "arco" | e,2. | b,,2. | e,2. | a,,2. | ais,,2. | ais,,2. | b,,2 fis,,4 | cis,2.\< | gis,,2. | fis,,2.\f\> | a,,2. | b,,2.\mf | b,,2. | e,2.~\upbow\> | e,2 r4\! | e,4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | a,,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | e,4 r4 r4 | }
% c047: 211 and 222 Hn 1 holds B (concord) instead of the V7's seventh A (A4 rose to B4 at 211-212; at 222 it doubled Bsn 1's A3): Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108,
%   horns + bassoons in soft four-part harmony, concords to the horns, discords to the bassoons; stationary parts to the brass.
% c047: 229-232 Vln II E5 and violas B3-A3 frame Cl 1's T head (it began in unison with the violas' held G sharp 4, its A4 rubbed against it, and its B4 sat on Vln II's held B4,
%   as at 197-200 before c039); 233-236 harp off-beats E4-G sharp 4-B4 (E4-A4-C5 at 235), above Bsn 1's T head G sharp 3-C4 inside Hn 3-4 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108, 112).
% c045: 224 timpani E p (it still read f from 209, a loud stroke in the diminuendo into the pp codetta). 229 Cl 2 p with Cl 1's p entry: the shared staff prints one dynamic.
% c067: 209 Fl 1 (and Fl 2's G sharp, same staff) mf under the violins' f: a flute on the lead violin line is marked a degree softer (Sebesky PDF 164).
% c081: T bars 10-11 (162, 178, 218, 350): the climax A is anticipated on beat 3 of bar 10 and tied over the bar line (was e4 gis2 | a4. gis8 fis4, now e4 gis4 a4~ | a4. gis8 fis4).
%   The phrase's highest note becomes its longest and a syncopation, the only bar-line tie before the cadence (Aldwell-Schachter Unit 3 PDF 50: long and high notes take accent;
%   PDF 51: a note begun on a weak beat and held through the strong beat contradicts the metre; PDF 56, Ex. 3-19: the anticipation held over into the consonance, here I6 -> ii).
%   Dvorak 9/iii's E-major tune (Ob 1, 68-76) ties over five bar lines in nine bars. Theme change (third phrase only); violins re-bow on the A.
% c112: 219-220 violas hold E4 over ii7-ii6/5 into the cadential 6/4 at 221 (7th prepared at 218 and held in the voice that becomes the 4th; A-S PDF 177, 181-182, Ex. 12-8); removes the 5ths Hn 1 C#5-B4 / Vla F#4-E4 at 220-221.
% c174: 214-215 harp LH A sharp 1 with the basses (pedal A sharp after 213, A natural after 216), not C sharp 2 a minor third above them; 223-224 Vc and Bsn 2 hold B2, the fifth over E2, instead of G sharp 2, a major third over the bass with no fifth in the chord (Casanelles PDF 264, 274: harmonic series)
